s  really not understood actually what No  Deal means we often take questions from  viewers and have to say that when I said  that you were coming on the show we've  got a lot of questions in it's clearly a  subject matter that people are quite  concerned about I've just got one  question here from a man called  Tim Kerby and he wants to ask about what  happens if there are problems at the  borders in the event if a No Deal breaks  say he says what in season foods can  Britons farmers provide immediately and  can they supply of sufficient amounts of  it to feed the population well at  certain times of year yes absolutely we  can and certainly post per exit and teas  now we could have been producing much  more of our fruit and veg at home but of  course you know we've been very reliant  on people choosing to come here seasonal  workers coming here we've been the  preferred destination for people to come  and pick fruit and veg that is all now  put massively in jeopardy and at this  time of year I was with River food  organics in the week and guy Watson  telling me that they are totally reliant  on being able to bring in broccoli and  many other things at this time of year  from Spain so you know you can see by my  farm at the moment you know we've got a  really heavy frost it's minus seven  today a snow covering of course in the  spring summer autumn yes we can produce  a lot more but at this time of year it's  not a great time of year we're into  really the root vegetables leeks  Brussels sprouts people have got used to  a wide variety really having anything  they want at any time of year  seasonality now for consumers is really  sort of forgotten passed one way of  forcing it to be a Brussels Frances  phase and if Britain was self-sufficient  when will we run out of food well look  we produce approximately 60% of the food  that we consume here in the UK we have a  brilliant climate for growing food we  are you know the second largest producer  sheet meat in the world second to new  zealand so you know we are  self-sufficient in areas particularly  liquid milk the dairy industry you can't  import liquid milk of course but i think  food has been taken for granted for far  far too long and if you look at the role  of pharmacists so the little red tractor  logo that shows with the Union Jack that  it's been produced and reared in the UK  and many other assurance schemes like  the leaf mark RSPCA organic certified  what I said about this tariff situation  and bring  cheap imports in this is the key  question because it would undermine  British production  we are the third cheapest producer of  food in the world so the people that say  this is about cheaper food brexit  actually consumers in this country are  getting a great deal already food is  incredibly cheap many would say far too  cheap in the UK with British farmers and  growers taking less than 6% out of that  value so I really hope that food can  rise up the political agenda it's  absolutely Savage for us and No Deal I  cannot imagine how bad it would look  because we'd see a long-term future of  just bringing cheap imports in and  whenever I raised this with Liam Fox or  Michael Gove or the Prime Minister you  know there are no plans at the moment  we've said you know we want this put in  writing on our high standards of welfare  and environmental protection they  haven't put it in writing yet and we  continue to ask and it's a question that  absolutely must be answered before we  leave on the 29th of March that's  interesting so what have those people  said what's the prime minister said to  you what's the in Fox said to you in  those meetings what haven't they said  well we've had a lot of warm words on  this that you know we would lot look to  lower our standards we would not look to  bring food in that is produced to lower  standards but ultimately if we are going  to leave in an orderly way with a deal  if we are going to negotiate free trade  agreements across the world then this  has to be put in writing it has to be  legislated on and we have asked for  bringing a uk-wide group together to  agree what we would want on animal  welfare environmental protection and  food safety a black and white policy  statement and this is not about farmers  this is about consumers 66 million  consumers across the UK that really  value the standards of production we  want it put in writing and it still  hasn't been put in writing when we have  eight weeks to go  it is really simply not good enough you  paint quite a consoling picture saying  that knowdo will be savaged for the  industry and at the same time a lot of  farmers voted to leave the EU many of  them because they don't much like the  Common Agricultural Policy many of them  because they feel that the current  subsidies regime is unfair so are some  positives from  a brexit I mean could we start buying  more locally and actually valuing our  own produce a bit more I think if  nothing else at this moment government  understands agriculture and the food  system far better than it did I mean  that is a concern in itself and yes if  we leave in an orderly manner if we are  prepared to have agriculture as part of  those free trade agreements and not  sacrificed I mean just to put it into  context the CETA deal which is the  Canadian trade deal with the European  Union that took over ten years to  achieve and in the end it was brought to  a standstill by three Belgian dairy  farmers agriculture food is always the  last chapter in any trade deal to be  agreed and my huge worry is that we are  an economy now based on services and  financial services goods is only 20% of  the economy and although food and  farming is the largest and indeed only  remaining primary manufacturing sector  it has to feature in these free trade  agreements and we're just not getting  the assurance that we would like to have  now we're almost that time but there is  one question I really want to ask you  before I let you go inside and warm up  feel it's quite snowy where you are  you're the first ever female president  of the nfu and farming isn't  traditionally seen as you know a woman's  job if you read books to kids it's all  about the farmer and his wife so I just  wanted to ask you if you've experienced  much sexism on your way to the top no  not at all I mean it's a bizarre concept  really because I think of all industries  women have always been involved very  much partnership businesses here in the  UK we're all about family farming  businesses so it's generally a family  running that business women at the  backbone of those businesses and I guess  success for me will be when being a  woman isn't newsworthy oh very good  oh very good on so hopefully one point I  went out to be asking these questions  and thank you very much for coming on  the show today is appreciated thanks so  much
 

